--- reading it --------------------------------------------------------------------------
Result: PASS. A hung kernel, with no userspace left to do anything, was reset by the hardware in 24 s
without anyone touching the board, and the slot came back and confirmed itself.

The time is the margin the userspace feeder set (30 s, `-T 30`), not the 60 s of the kernel's own
timeout: once the feeder has claimed the device it also programs the timeout, so a kernel that dies
after confirmation is caught within 30 s of the last feed. The 60 s figure applies to a kernel that
hangs before userspace has claimed the watchdog. contracts/boot-interface.md had them as one row
and is corrected.

The clock reading in this capture shows the RTC counting across resets (2000-01-01 04:16 at boot,
03:37 at the previous one): the RTC keeps running while the board has power and only the system
clock is reset, which is why ota-clock still has to raise it every boot.

Still NOT on record: scenario B (a slot that never confirms: a reset about every two minutes, the
attempt counter walking 3 -> 2 -> 1 -> 0, then the other slot; SC-014, three trials).

